<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Uhud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/625">625</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Uhud was fought in March 625 at Mount Uhud, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, between the Muslims under Muhammad and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> led by Abu Sufyan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">Archers</a> who left their posts allowed a Meccan cavalry charge under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, and the Muslims were defeated, with Muhammad wounded and his uncle Hamza killed. The Meccans did not follow up their victory.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Muslims migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, hostilities with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> grew, largely because of Muslim raids on Meccan caravans. In 624 the Muslims defeated a more numerous Meccan force at Badr, where several Quraysh leaders were killed, including Amr ibn Hisham. Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, chosen as their leader, vowed revenge, and wealthy Meccans who had lost relatives gave money for another expedition. He mobilised more than 3,000 men against Medina.</p><p>Muhammad had meant to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and use its fortifications, but many Muslims who had missed Badr wanted open battle, and he gave way. Shortly before the battle Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, chief of the Khazraj, withdrew with 300 men. The Muslim force of around 700 took position on the slopes of Mount Uhud, which protected its rear. Muhammad posted 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> on a nearby hill to guard the flank against the Meccan cavalry and ordered them not to leave their places.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle took place on 23 March 625. It opened with duels in which the Meccan standard-bearer Talha ibn Abi Talha was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, and others of his family who took up the banner fell one after another. In the general engagement the Muslims swept through the Meccan ranks. Believing the battle won, most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> left the hill to collect spoils. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, commanding the Meccan cavalry, attacked the few who stayed and then struck the Muslim flank and rear.</p><p>Many Muslims were killed, among them Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib, Muhammad's uncle. Rumour spread that Muhammad had died, but he had been wounded in the face. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a> rallied the troops, and a counter-charge drove Khalid's men from the slopes. The Meccan cavalry could not climb the slope, and Abu Sufyan returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> without attacking the wounded Muslims again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Scholars differ on the result. Chase F. Robinson writes that a disheartening Muslim defeat is clear enough, while William Montgomery Watt notes that the Meccans failed to destroy Muhammad and his followers. The Qur'an, in a verse revealed soon after, attributes the setback to disobedience and desire for loot and treats it as a test. Abu Sufyan then sought alliances with nomadic tribes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> returned in 627 with a larger confederate force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench">Battle of the Trench</a>. Khalid ibn al-Walid's leadership at Uhud was an early sign of the career that made him one of the best-known Arab generals of the later conquests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Uhud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The community fought Pre-Islamic Mecca at Badr (624), <b>Uhud</b> (625) and the Trench (627), and expelled or defeated the Jewish tribes of Medina.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-state-of-medina">Islamic state of Medina</a></li></ul></section>
